==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|dismal}}, {{m|enm|dismale}}, from {{der|en|xno|dismal}}, from {{der|en|fro|([[li]]) [[dis]] [[mals]]|t=(the) bad days}}, from {{der|en|ML.|[[diēs]] [[malī]]|t=bad days}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɪzməl/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-dismal.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪzməl|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{cap|disastrous}}, [[calamitous]].
#: {{syn|en|cataclysmic|catastrophic|ruinous}}
# Disappointingly [[inadequate]].
#: {{syn|en|meager|paltry|Thesaurus:inadequate}}
#: {{ux|en|He received a '''dismal''' compensation.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=April 22, 2012|author=Sam Sheringham|work=BBC Sport
|title=[http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/17718147 Liverpool 0-1 West Brom]
|passage=Liverpool's efforts thereafter had an air of desperation as their '''dismal''' 2012 league form continued.}}
# Causing despair; [[gloomy]] and [[bleak]].
#: {{syn|en|desolate|dreary|Thesaurus:cheerless}}
#: {{ux|en|The storm made for a '''dismal''' weekend}}
# [[depressing|Depressing]], dreary, cheerless.
#: {{syn|en|sorrowful|mournful|Thesaurus:sad}}
#: {{ux|en|She was lost in '''dismal''' thoughts of despair}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=12|passage=So, after a spell, he decided to make the best of it and shoved us into the front parlor. 'Twas a '''dismal''' sort of place, with hair wreaths, and wax fruit, and tin lambrekins, and land knows what all. It looked like a tomb and smelt pretty nigh as musty and dead-and-gone.}}

